The International Airport Terminal

To understand why Ingress exists, consider an international airport:

Without Ingress (The NodePort/LoadBalancer approach): Imagine if every airline terminal and gate required its own dedicated highway, its own security gate, and its own immigration checkpoint from the outside world. Building 50 separate highways for 50 airlines would be expensive, chaotic, and impossible to manage.

With Ingress: The airport has one main entrance highway and one central security gate (a single public IP / Load Balancer). Inside the terminal, an electronic departure board and directional signs (the Ingress rules) inspect your ticket and guide you:

Passengers going to /flights/domestic head to Terminal A.

Passengers going to /flights/international head to Terminal B.

VIPs boarding vip.airport.com are routed to the Executive Lounge.

In Kubernetes, Ingress is that central entry point and directional router for all HTTP/HTTPS web traffic.


The E-Commerce Platform Example

Imagine running an e-commerce platform (shopzone.com) built with microservices:

[shopzone.com/products](https://shopzone.com/products) → Product Catalog Service

[shopzone.com/cart](https://shopzone.com/cart) → Shopping Cart & Checkout Service

api.shopzone.com → Backend REST API

admin.shopzone.com → Internal Admin Dashboard

Without Ingress, each service would need a dedicated cloud provider Load Balancer
(e.g., AWS ALB/NLB at roughly $20–$30/month each), resulting in separate external IP addresses and fragmented SSL certificates.

With Ingress, you pay for one cloud Load Balancer and one public IP address. 
The Ingress Controller terminates HTTPS, checks your SSL certificate once, inspects the requested domain and URL path, and forwards the packet directly to the correct internal ClusterIP service.


